= October Revolution

In the late hours of 7 November 1917, the 2nd Soviet Congress was called to order, amidst the sounds of cannon fire off in the distance. The fall of the Winter Palace was under way, and with it, the arrest of the Provisional Government, which had struggled to govern Russia the past several months. 

Our unilateral arrest of the previous government, before the Congress could give any sort of sanction, has proved the tipping point for the other socialist parties, long exacerbated by our actions. In an attempt to salvage the situation, the <span style="color: var(--left-m-color);">Menshevik Internationalist</span> Iulius Martov has called for a cessation of the warfare in the streets, and immediate negotiations for the formation of all-socialist coalition government—a proposal that passes unanimously.

However, the events of the evening have made broad cooperation a fleeting dream. A succession of <span style="color: var(--m-color);">Mensheviks</span> and <span style="color: var(--sr-color);">Socialist-Revolutionaries</span> speakers take to the floor to condemn us as usurpers, before ultimately walking out; our own speakers condemn those parties as servants of capital and reaction. Finally, an inflammatory resolution is proposed by Trotsky, which would formally condemn the parties outright, in order to properly send them off "into the dustbin of history". Even those like Martov are unable to stomach this, and soon walk out too.

Under these auspices, Lenin reads out his manifesto, declaring the following:<br><br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• Transfer of supreme power into the hands of the Congress,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; and local soviets everywhere.<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• Intentions to create an immediate peace in the Great War,<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; without annexations or indemnities.<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• Transfer of land to peasant committees.<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• Complete democratization of the army and defense of soldiers' rights.<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• Workers' control over production and bread in the cities.<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• The right of self-determination to all nationalities.<br> 
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• Timely convocation of the Constituent Assembly.<br>

It is an ambitious political platform, but more than that it is a declaration to the workers of the entire world: the revolution has begun! 

In the meantime, we will have to find a way to govern...

